WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.380
[MUSIC]

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:13.280
Hi, today we'll talk about wine-making,
but not really

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:19.100
about the technology of wine-making or
how to choose a good bottle of wine --

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.310
something that I know little about.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:26.700
Instead, we'll talk about
the genetic basis of wine making.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:32.330
Humans figured out how to make
wine thousands of years ago, but

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:38.940
with all due respect the biggest star
of winemaking is not Robert Mondavi.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.350
The biggest star of winemaking is yeast.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:47.000
However, until recently, it
was not clear how yeast

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.300
accomplished the very complex
process of wine making.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.620
And today we will learn
how clustering algorithms

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:00.280
help scientists to figure
out how yeast made wine.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.130
I'll start with the question:

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:07.170
Which domesticated animal
is missing in this slide?

00:01:07.170 --> 00:01:11.270
And of course you already know the answer
since the whole lecture is about yeast.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.650
It is yeast.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:17.365
In fact, ten years ago, scientists
found an Armenian cave

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:22.110
equipped with old wine making
equipment including

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.530
even stone cups for wine drinking.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:33.980
And my next question is: Why do we store
grapes in sealed containers to make wine?

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:39.860
It doesn't appear to be necessary
because yeasts live on grapes,

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:46.360
and they have natural abilities to
transform glucose in grapes into ethanol.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.280
And therefore, it looks like the only
thing that is needed to make wine

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.580
is simply to crush grapes and
let yeast do their job.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:00.140
However, it's not that easy.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:05.010
It turns out that yeast have
developed another amazing ability.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:10.370
After they transform glucose into ethanol
and run out of glucose,

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:16.306
they can invert their metabolism and
feed on the ethanol they just produced.

00:02:16.306 --> 00:02:19.840
And thus, they would ruin the wine

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:24.410
that they just made.

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:32.110
And this change - reversion of metabolism-
is called the diauxic shift.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:36.925
And our goal today
is to figure out which yeast genes

00:02:36.925 --> 00:02:40.940
are implicated in the diauxic shift.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:46.450
But before we answer this question,
I wanted to ask you: What does it take,

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:50.000
biochemically, to convert
glucose into ethanol?

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.290
Imagine the time,
over 100 million years ago,

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:59.550
when the first fruit bearing
plants appeared on earth.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:04.360
At that time,
an organism that would invent a way to

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:10.420
metabolize glucose in fruit would
have an enormous evolutionary benefit,

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:14.380
particularly if it would
convert glucose into ethanol,

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:19.666
because ethanol is a poison for
most of the competitor of the organism.

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:26.480
However, transforming glucose
into ethanol is not a small feat,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:32.270
let alone metabolizing
ethanol afterwards.

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:35.200
It is very complex by
chemical reaction and

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:39.630
it requires creating
new metabolic pathways.

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:44.340
Essentially, a revolution in the
biochemical abilities of the organism.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:50.440
We know that genes mutate and
can build new functions, but

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:55.620
in this case, developing one, two,
three, even ten genes here and

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:59.800
there, won't accomplish the task of
building a new metabolic pathway.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:05.850
Let's create a certain
evolutionary puzzle and

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:11.550
we'll return back to Susumu Ohno,
who's work we started in the
Genome Rearrangement chapter.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:16.050
Susumu Ohno came up with
the Random Breakage Model,

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:20.150
that has become the dogma of

00:04:20.150 --> 00:04:26.470
chromosome evolution study for 30 years,
until it was refuted just 10 years ago.

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.510
So why do you bring Susumu Ohno

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:31.810
if his random breakage
model turned out to be wrong?

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:42.560
Because he came up with another evolutionary
model that had a very different fate.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:46.550
He argued that big leaps
in evolution would not

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:51.010
have been possible without
whole genome duplication.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:55.540
Essentially, doubling the number of
chromosomes in the genome and thus

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:56.230
doubling the number of genes.

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.000
And whole genome duplication, in difference to

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.990
the Random Breakage Model, first was
heavily criticized by biologists.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.900
They did not believe in it for 30 years,

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:12.650
until it was confirmed just ten
years ago, roughly at the same time as

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:15.290
when the Random Breakage Model
was confirmed.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:20.179
But how does whole genome duplication help

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:24.200
to invent new metabolic pathways?

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:27.250
Well, right after the whole
genome duplication,

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:31.120
every gene will be present in two copies.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:36.400
One copy will be free to evolve whatever
new revolutionary function is needed.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:39.060
For example,
creating a new metabolic pathway,

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.670
while another copy of the gene
will simply support the life of

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:48.020
the existing organism; do whatever function that
it was supposed to do before the duplication.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:54.272
So, after we introduce the biological

00:05:54.272 --> 00:05:58.751
background for the diauxic shift,

00:05:58.751 --> 00:06:04.002
our next goal is to explain how biologists

00:06:04.002 --> 00:06:10.654
measure gene expression
to study the diauxic shift.

